## Why This Is Asked
Tests proficiency designing fine-grained, scalable access controls for multi-tenant data lakes using ABAC, tags, and policy syntax.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tags and aws:PrincipalTag
- Resource policies with aws:RequestTag and stringEquals conditions
- S3 bucket prefixes per tenant and least privilege
- Testing cross-tenant access and tag revocation

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy (IAM role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Permission policy
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Bucket policy (alternative strict guard)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"s3:ExistingObjectTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tags and ensure no stale access remains?
- How would you integrate with CI to validate policies in a sandbox before prod?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical serverless design, idempotency, error handling, and IAM least privilege in a real-world image workflow.

## Key Concepts

- S3 event triggers and Lambda integration
- Idempotent design using a dedupe key stored in DynamoDB
- Least-privilege IAM roles for S3, Lambda, and DynamoDB
- Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) setup for failed events

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal idempotent Lambda outline
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
// image processing omitted
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const rec = event.Records[0];
  const key = recs.s3.object.key;
  const jobKey = key;
  const exists = await ddb.get({TableName:'processed', Key:{jobKey}}).promise();
  if (exists.Item) return {status:'skipped'};
  // resize and store output placeholder
  await ddb.put({TableName:'processed', Item:{jobKey, t: Date.now()}}).promise();
  return {status:'done'};
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency under concurrent uploads?
- How would you monitor DLQ failures and ensure timely retries?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of Secrets Manager with a Lambda-backed API, rotation fundamentals, and validation of continuity.

## Key Concepts
- Secrets Manager secrets and rotation
- Least-privilege IAM roles
- Rotation Lambda workflow
- End-to-end testing without downtime

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const sm = new AWS.SecretsManager();
async function getSecret(name){
  const r = await sm.getSecretValue({SecretId:name}).promise();
  return JSON.parse(r.SecretString);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle rotation failure and alerting?
- How would you extend to multiple environments (dev/stage/prod)?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses capability to design tenant isolation, data residency, and governance at scale using AWS services.

## Key Concepts
- Region-bound storage for residency compliance
- Fine-grained access with Lake Formation and IAM conditions
- Envelope encryption with per-tenant CMKs
- Automated onboarding/offboarding and disaster recovery

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-eu-tenantA",
        "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-eu-tenantA/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-12345"
        }
