## Why This Is Asked

This validates practical isolation in a shared data lake, ensuring least privilege and scalable onboarding. It also tests policy layering and auditing capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- IAM roles per tenant with strict resource boundaries
- Trust policies tied to the tenant app identity
- S3 prefix-scoped permissions with bucket or IAM policy conditions
- Onboarding automation for new tenants and centralized governance
- Cross-tenant test plans and auditing hooks

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake",
      "Condition": {"StringLike": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-<tid>/*"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake/tenant-<tid>/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "<tid>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::<account>:role/TenantRole-<tid>",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "<tid>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this model to support cross-region backups without cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you automate onboarding for new tenants without touching code (CI/CD policy issuance, audit log integration)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a simple, observable, and secure event-driven workflow with minimal services.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event notifications
- IAM least-privilege policies for Lambda
- Lambda with external libraries (sharp)
- DynamoDB PutItem basics
- Dead Letter Queue for error handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Lambda handler using sharp
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
const sharp = require('sharp');
exports.handler = async (ev) => {
  const bucket = 'photos-uploads';
  const key = ev.Records[0].s3.object.key;
  const obj = await s3.getObject({Bucket: bucket, Key: key}).promise();
  const thumb = await sharp(obj.Body).resize(800,600).toBuffer();
  const thumbKey = key.split('.').slice(0, -1).join('.') + '_thumb.jpg';
  await s3.putObject({Bucket: 'photos-thumbs', Key: thumbKey, Body: thumb}).promise();
  await ddb.put({TableName: 'Photos', Item: {imageKey: key, thumbKey, ts: Date.now(), size: obj.ContentLength}}).promise();
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test idempotency for repeated uploads?
- How would you handle very large images to avoid memory issues?
